>

<p>
  Certificate management touches the operationally sensitive parts of
  a business: production hostnames, deployment automation, the keys
  that prove your identity on the public internet. In any environment
  with a compliance regime — SOC 2, ISO 27001, PCI, internal change-
  management — the question "who changed what, when" is going to be
  asked.
</p>

<p>
  CertMate writes one structured audit row per state-changing
  operation. The schema is stable, the queries are cheap, and the
  data is enough to answer the questions an auditor actually asks.
</p>

<h2>The schema</h2>

<p>
  Every audit entry is a JSON document with a fixed shape:
</p>

<pre><code>{`{
  "timestamp": "2026-05-17T18:00:00Z",
  "operation": "create" | "renew" | "revoke" | "delete" |
               "download" | "deploy" | "config_change" | ...,
  "resource_type": "certificate" | "client_cert" | "backup" |
                   "dns_account" | "setting" | "deploy_hook",
  "resource_id": "example.com",
  "status": "success" | "error",
  "user": "alice@example.com",
  "ip_address": "10.0.0.42",
  "details": {
    "common_name": "example.com",
    "san_domains": ["*.example.com"],
    "dns_provider": "cloudflare",
    "dns_account_id": "production",
    "serial_number": "04:5e:a1:..."
  },
  "error": null
}`}</code></pre>

<p>
  The fields worth specifically calling out:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <code>user</code> is written verbatim by the caller &mdash;
    <code>user or 'system'</code>. For an API key it is
    <code>api_key:&lt;name&gt;</code>, for a session it is the username. There is
    no join and no email: the key's <code>created_by</code> records which admin
    minted it, and stays on the key record rather than being copied onto every
    audit row.
  </li>
  <li>
    <code>ip_address</code> is the source IP of the HTTP request — or
    the loopback when CertMate's scheduler is acting on its own
    (renewals). Useful to distinguish "human action" from "scheduled
    job".
  </li>
  <li>
    <code>details</code> is a free-form object whose shape depends on
    the operation. Renewals carry the new serial; deploys carry the
    hook id; config changes carry a diff of the field that changed.
  </li>
  <li>
    <code>error</code> is null on success, populated on failure with
    the upstream error verbatim. This is the field your audit query
    grep against when investigating an incident.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>What gets written</h2>

<p>
  CertMate's auditor module exposes specific log methods for the
  events that matter:
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Event</th><th>When it fires</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>certificate_created</code></td><td>First issuance for a domain.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>certificate_renewed</code></td><td>Successful renewal (new serial number).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>certificate_revoked</code></td><td>Revocation against the CA's OCSP endpoint.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>certificate_downloaded</code></td><td>Anyone fetched the cert / key over the API.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>batch_operation</code></td><td>Bulk issue/renew/revoke kicked off.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>api_request</code></td><td>Non-state-changing API calls (optional, configurable).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>deploy_hook_changed</code></td><td>A hook was created, edited or removed. Note: hook <em>invocation</em> is not audited &mdash; the run and its output land in the deploy history, not the chain.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>settings_changed</code></td><td>Settings and DNS accounts. CA changes are <code>ca_provider_changed</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>authz_denied</code></td><td>A request rejected on role or domain scope.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>
  The full method list lives in <code>modules/core/audit.py</code> &mdash;
  eighteen <code>log_*</code> methods, from <code>log_certificate_created</code>
  to <code>log_authz_denied</code>. The non-obvious one is
  <code>log_authz_denied</code>: a request rejected on role or domain scope
  writes an audit row with the attempted resource. That is what you grep when
  an auditor asks &ldquo;show me the unauthorized access attempts&rdquo;.
</p>

<h2>Queryable patterns</h2>

<p>
  There is no filtered query endpoint. <code>GET /api/web/audit-logs</code>
  (admin) returns the most recent entries and takes one parameter,
  <code>limit</code>, capped at 1000 — you filter client-side.
  <code>GET /api/audit/export</code> (admin) returns the signed, independently
  verifiable bundle, optionally sliced by <code>from_seq</code> /
  <code>to_seq</code>. The patterns auditors actually ask for, answered against
  what exists:
</p>

<h3>"Who renewed cert X in the last 90 days?"</h3>

<pre><code>{`curl -G http://localhost:8000/api/web/audit-logs \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  --data-urlencode "limit=1000" \\
  | jq '[.[] | select(.operation == "renew"
        and .resource_id == "example.com")]'`}</code></pre>

<h3>"Show me every failed renewal in the last week"</h3>

<pre><code>{`curl -G http://localhost:8000/api/web/audit-logs \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  --data-urlencode "limit=1000" \\
  | jq '[.[] | select(.operation == "renew" and .status == "error")]'`}</code></pre>

<p>
  If a thousand entries is not enough reach for the window you need, take the
  export bundle instead — it is the whole chain, and it verifies off the box.
</p>

<h3>"Who has access to issue against *.example.com?"</h3>

<p>
  This isn't really an audit-log question — it's an authorization
  question. The answer comes from the API key registry:
</p>

<pre><code>{`curl http://localhost:8000/api/keys \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  | jq '.keys[] | select(
        .role == "operator" and
        ((.allowed_domains // []) | any(. as $d | "example.com" | inside($d)))
      )'`}</code></pre>

<p>
  The route is <code>/api/keys</code> — <code>/api/auth/*</code> is the OIDC
  surface — and it answers with <code>&#123;"keys": [...]&#125;</code>, so the
  filter walks <code>.keys[]</code> rather than the top level.
</p>

<h2>Retention and export</h2>

<p>
  The audit trail is a line-delimited JSON file, not a database, and it is
  bounded by size rather than by age. A rotating handler keeps
  <code>CERTMATE_AUDIT_LOG_MAX_BYTES</code> per file (10 MB by default) across
  <code>CERTMATE_AUDIT_LOG_BACKUP_COUNT</code> files (5), so roughly 60 MB
  before the oldest entries fall off the end. There is no retention-in-days
  setting: if your compliance requirement is expressed in time rather than
  bytes, ship the entries somewhere that keeps them.
</p>

<p>
  Export the log periodically to your SIEM / log aggregator so the
  audit trail survives a CertMate disaster:
</p>

<pre><code>{`# Tail the audit log file directly (JSONL format)
tail -f /app/logs/audit/certificate_audit.log | \\
  vector --config /etc/vector/certmate.toml

# Or pull the signed bundle, sliced since your last run
curl -G http://localhost:8000/api/audit/export \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  --data-urlencode "from_seq=$LAST_SEQ"`}</code></pre>

<p>
  The on-disk format is line-delimited JSON — every line is one entry
  — which lines up with how virtually every log shipper expects to
  ingest data. <code>jq</code>, Splunk, Elastic, Datadog, Loki,
  Vector: they all read this without configuration.
</p>

<h2>What's not in the audit log (and where to look)</h2>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Configuration before the change.</strong> CertMate writes
    a <code>config_change</code> row with the field that changed and
    the new value. The previous value is in the latest
    <code>backup_*.zip</code> — CertMate auto-creates a backup
    immediately before every config change, exactly so you can
    answer "what did it look like before?"
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The bytes of the private key.</strong> The audit log
    records that a key was generated or rotated and what its
    fingerprint is. The actual bytes live in{" "}
    <code>/app/certificates/&lt;domain&gt;/privkey.pem</code> with
    mode 600 and never enter the audit stream.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>HTTP request bodies.</strong> CertMate logs that a
    request happened, what user, against what resource, with what
    outcome. It doesn't log the full request body — that would be a
    PII-exfiltration risk in a multi-tenant deployment.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Wiring this into a compliance program</h2>

<p>
  The basics, in order of effort:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>
    Size the rotation for the window you need
    (<code>CERTMATE_AUDIT_LOG_MAX_BYTES</code> x
    <code>CERTMATE_AUDIT_LOG_BACKUP_COUNT</code>), set
    <code>API_BEARER_TOKEN</code> to a long random value, and set up at
    least one operator-role API key per human who needs CertMate access
    (don't share the admin token).
  </li>
  <li>
    Ship the audit log to your SIEM with a real-time tail (Vector,
    Filebeat, Promtail) — file path{" "}
    <code>/app/logs/audit/certificate_audit.log</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    Wire alerts on <code>auth_failure</code> bursts (signal of
    credential probing) and <code>renew/error</code> repeats (signal
    of a broken DNS path before the cert expires).
  </li>
  <li>
    Run CertMate's signed container image (every release ships with
    cosign signature + SLSA L3 provenance — see the release page) so
    the binary you're auditing is the binary that left CI.
  </li>
</ol>

<p>
  None of this is novel. It's the same audit posture you'd expect
  from any well-run operational tool. The point is that CertMate
  ships it as a contract, not as something you bolt on.
</p>

